(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is facility-associated scabies a particular concern?","choices":["Residents are children under twelve, in whom the infestation spreads through shared headgear","Residents are homeless or displaced, in whom infestation persists through unwashed clothing","Residents travel frequently, carrying the organism between facilities in luggage and bedding","Residents are elderly and immunosuppressed, and a hospital epidemic can follow their admission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is difficult to eradicate once healthcare workers are infested. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for vitiligo involving more than 5% of the body surface?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid applied twice daily to all affected areas of skin","Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented skin","Systemic immunosuppression with a calcineurin inhibitor taken orally","Phototherapy, with narrow band ultraviolet B preferred over PUVA"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — PUVA carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What should be done with a low-positive herpes simplex type 2 serology result?","choices":["Confirm it with a second method when one is available","Accept it as diagnostic and begin suppressive therapy","Discard it and repeat the identical assay in six months","Confirm it with an immunoglobulin M level on the same sample"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — low-positive results may be false positives. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for Rocky Mountain spotted fever?","choices":["Immunosuppression, renal failure, alcoholism and injection drug use","Obesity, diabetes and nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Male sex, adults aged 40 to 64, children under 10, and rural dwelling","Female sex, adults over 65 years, urban dwelling, and winter exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is prevalent in southeastern and south central states in spring and early summer. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man living in a shelter has intense itching with linear excoriations across his back, neck, shoulders and waist, and patches of darker pigmentation where older lesions have healed. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scabies of the trunk and back","Body louse infestation","Head louse infestation","Pubic louse infestation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that distribution with post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases is characteristic. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the deck say about eradicating human papillomavirus with wart therapy?","choices":["Salicylic acid eradicates it reliably when used for six weeks","Surgical excision eradicates it reliably in immunocompetent patients","No therapy eradicates it with certainty, and recurrence can occur","Cryotherapy eradicates it reliably, so recurrence does not occur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — set expectations accordingly. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"With what other anomalies can naevus spilus be associated?","choices":["Anomalies of vascular, central nervous system or connective tissue origin","Anomalies of the endocrine system with insulin resistance and hyperandrogenism","Anomalies of the gastrointestinal tract with malabsorption and coeliac disease","Anomalies of the haematologic system with monoclonal gammopathy and myeloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is a variant of congenital naevus present at birth or in the first years of life. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has impetigo covering much of one arm and both cheeks, too extensive for topical therapy. He has no drug allergies. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral ciprofloxacin, which covers resistant organisms","Oral fluconazole, which covers candidal superinfection","Oral cephalexin, the drug of choice in children","Oral doxycycline, which covers both likely organisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What is the most important modifiable association of perioral dermatitis?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid exposure","Sun exposure","Overgrowth of Malassezia yeast","Nickel exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and treatment begins by stopping it, with a warning that the eruption may temporarily worsen on withdrawal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which single feature is characteristic of the molluscum lesion?","choices":["Central umbilication","An advancing scaly border","A thrombosed capillary dot","A shallow painful ulcer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck calls it characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a large keratoacanthoma on the nasal tip. Which approach does the lecture indicate?","choices":["Standard elliptical excision with five-millimetre margins regardless of site","Cryotherapy, which spares the nasal cartilage","Intralesional methotrexate as definitive treatment","Mohs surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is indicated for large or recurrent lesions, and for lesions in anatomic areas with cosmetic or functional considerations. This lesion is both. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which measure is specifically advised against in the management of sunburn?","choices":["Applying aloe vera or a soy-based topical moisturiser","Giving oral or intravenous fluids for systemic symptoms","Popping blisters, because intact blisters are protective","Starting a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug early in the course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cool rather than cold water is also specified. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with freckles across her cheeks asks what will reduce them. Which is the most appropriate treatment plan?","choices":["Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy given three times weekly over a five week course","Sun protection and counselling first, then topical depigmenting agents, with intense pulsed light or laser if needed","Cryotherapy to each lesion with five to ten second freezes, repeated over several sessions as needed","Excision with negative margins for the largest lesions, with observation of the remainder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids and botanicals are the topical options. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"After drainage, the patient asks how to stop the problem recurring. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Avoid sitting for more than two hours at a time and use a specialty cushion","Apply an over-the-counter salicylic acid preparation twice weekly","Avoid ear piercing and other cosmetic procedures","Keep the area clean and free of debris, and consider shaving or laser hair therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and seek care promptly if an abscess occurs again. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which slides did Professor Jaquith name out loud as really important, and what are they?","choices":["The necrotizing fasciitis red flags","The acne treatment ladder, slides 32 and 33","The MRSA risk factor list, slides 44 and 45","The cellulitis versus erysipelas comparison"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — she said 'make sure you know this', which is as direct a flag as the lecture gives. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 15:40"},{"q":"Which trait is shared by the oral agents named across this lecture for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus skin infection?","choices":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin and doxycycline recur as the core options","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin and amoxicillin-clavulanate recur as the core options","Penicillin V, erythromycin and clarithromycin recur as the core options","Mupirocin, retapamulin and fusidic acid recur as the core options"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with linezolid and ciprofloxacin added in particular settings. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has a flat, blanchable, pink-purple patch on one cheek that has been present since birth and stops sharply at the midline. His parents ask what can be done about it. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Oral propranolol as first line, with corticosteroids as an alternative","Surgical excision in infancy before it thickens","Observation only, because it fades during the first year","No treatment is required; tinted waterproof makeup, or pulsed dye laser"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the laser selectively destroys the superficial target vessels. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man wishes to treat male-pattern hair loss and asks about finasteride. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The sexual side effects are permanent once they occur","It is most effective at the frontal hairline","It can safely be shared with a female partner who is also experiencing hair thinning at the crown","About 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, and these effects reverse when the drug is stopped"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It works best combined with minoxidil, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A patient with known Kaposi sarcoma has marked lower limb oedema but only three visible skin lesions. How should the oedema be interpreted?","choices":["Marked oedema is not a feature of Kaposi sarcoma at any stage and points instead to venous thrombosis","Marked oedema indicates the classic rather than the epidemic form, and its severity gauges the disease burden","Marked oedema can occur even with few or no visible lesions, so its severity must not be used to gauge disease burden","Marked oedema indicates a correspondingly low disease burden, consistent with the small number of visible skin lesions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the skin can look almost clear while disease is extensive. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"When may radiation be used for a keloid?","choices":["Only in patients who cannot tolerate silicone sheeting or compression garments","During the first two weeks after the keloid has been excised","As a first-line treatment before any surgery is attempted","Only once intralesional steroid and compression have both failed over a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is an adjunct to surgery rather than a primary treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has cellulitis of the thigh with a central area of purulence. Which antibiotic choice is most appropriate?","choices":["Penicillin V, with clindamycin if the patient is penicillin allergic","Topical mupirocin, with oral cephalexin if lesions become widespread","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, clindamycin or linezolid","Dicloxacillin or cephalexin, with clindamycin if penicillin allergic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — purulent cellulitis prompts cover for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman is admitted with toxic epidermal necrolysis. She has a known lymphoma, a heart rate of 132, detachment of 35% of her body surface, blood urea nitrogen of 34 mg/dL, bicarbonate of 17 mEq/L and glucose of 190 mg/dL. Which score should be calculated and what does it indicate?","choices":["A SCORTEN of 2, indicating a predicted mortality of about 12%","A SCORTEN of 7, indicating a predicted mortality of about 90%","A SCORTEN of 6, indicating a predicted mortality of about 90%","A SCORTEN of 4, indicating a predicted mortality of about 35%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — age, malignancy, heart rate, detachment, urea and bicarbonate each score, and glucose is below threshold. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"When should a lichen simplex chronicus plaque be biopsied?","choices":["When emollients alone have failed to settle the plaque completely","When it is atypical, unilateral, nodular, ulcerated or treatment-resistant","When it has been present for longer than six weeks in any location","When the patient reports that the itch is worse at night than by day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the purpose is to exclude neoplasia or another inflammatory disorder. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 182"},{"q":"What triggers the majority of erythema multiforme cases?","choices":["Sulfonamide antibiotics, accounting for over 50% of cases","Mycoplasma pneumoniae, accounting for over 50% of cases","Idiopathic causes, accounting for over 50% of cases","Herpes simplex virus, accounting for over 50% of cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Other infectious causes include Mycoplasma pneumoniae, histoplasmosis, Epstein-Barr virus and coxsackievirus. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is first-line therapy for pityriasis versicolor, and what is one named regimen?","choices":["Oral terbinafine taken for a course of two weeks","Topical nystatin applied twice daily for a fortnight","Topical therapy; selenium sulfide daily for seven days","Oral fluconazole for seven days, in every case regardless of extent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — regimens vary by product. Ketoconazole shampoo or cream, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox and topical terbinafine are the other options. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which cutaneous T-cell lymphoma features worsen prognosis, and which carries the best?","choices":["Patch and plaque disease worsen it; tumour-stage disease carries the better prognosis","Only lymphadenopathy worsens it; skin findings do not affect prognosis","Prognosis is uniformly poor regardless of stage","Tumours, erythroderma and lymphadenopathy worsen it; limited patch disease does not reduce survival"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — survival is not reduced in limited patch-stage disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is finasteride, and what are its cautions?","choices":["An oral antiandrogen used first line in women","A topical 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor applied to the crown, whose effects on libido and erectile function are permanent and do not reverse at all when the drug is stopped","An oral 5-alpha-reductase type 2 inhibitor; about 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, reversible on stopping, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy","A topical vasodilator effective mainly at the crown"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it works best combined with minoxidil. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"Which supportive measure is described as one of the most important considerations in alopecia areata?","choices":["Referral to a wig service before starting any medical therapy","Psychological support, with support groups offered","Referral to endocrinology for thyroid function assessment","Referral for hair transplantation early in the disease course"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the visible nature of the condition carries real psychological weight. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"Which lesion features make early squamous cell carcinoma the distinction to exclude in an actinic keratosis?","choices":["Rough, flat, flesh-coloured, and more apparent by touch than it is by sight","Pearly, translucent, with telangiectasias","Scaly, symmetric and under two millimetres","Thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent or recurrent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck's full list of features that shift the concern to carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has an ulcer on his ear that has not healed in three months. The base is firm and indurated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A non-healing ulcer with an indurated base is one of the presentations the deck lists, and induration is on its red-flag list. The ear is also one of its named high-risk sites — with mucosal surfaces, lip, scalp, temple, nose and genitalia — all of which carry higher recurrence and metastatic risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which systemic features can accompany cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["Headaches, fever and lymphangitis","Cramping abdominal pain and muscle spasm","Cranial nerve palsy and meningitis","Aplastic anaemia and memory impairment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pain and swelling peak at forty-eight to seventy-two hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which laboratory and imaging studies are appropriate in classic erysipelas?","choices":["Computed tomography of the affected limb to define the depth of tissue involvement","Punch biopsy of the leading edge to distinguish erysipelas from contact dermatitis","None routinely — the diagnosis is clinical, and blood and tissue cultures have very low yield","Blood and tissue cultures in every case, since organism identification guides the antibiotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis and raised inflammatory markers are common but not diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a purulent wound and the clinician wants to know both what is growing and what will treat it. Which test should be ordered?","choices":["Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Fungal culture of the wound surface","Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Tzanck smear of the wound base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the culture names the organism and the sensitivity directs the antibiotic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"One of that 71-year-old's scalp lesions is now thickened, indurated and has bled twice. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat with liquid nitrogen cryotherapy and review in three months, since these are expected features","Begin field-directed fluorouracil to the whole scalp and review at the end of the course","Reassure and review in a year, since actinic keratoses commonly involute without treatment","Order a shave or punch biopsy, because induration and bleeding raise concern for squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those features are not part of a typical actinic keratosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does the imidazole class work, and which two examples does the deck give?","choices":["It blocks synthesis of chitin in the fungal cell wall; nystatin and amphotericin","It inhibits fungal deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis; griseofulvin and fluconazole","It blocks ergosterol synthesis; clotrimazole and ketoconazole","It destroys the fungal cell membrane directly; terbinafine and naftifine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ergosterol is a vital component of the fungal cell membrane, so blocking it halts growth and replication. The names end in -azole. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient with recurrent genital herpes simplex asks how to reduce the risk of infecting his partner. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Condoms eliminate transmission entirely, so antiviral therapy adds nothing for a patient who uses them","Neither suppressive antivirals nor condoms affect transmission at all, so the only option available is to avoid sexual contact permanently","Suppressive valacyclovir lowers transmission and condoms reduce risk, but neither eliminates it; avoid contact during the prodrome and while lesions are active","Suppressive valacyclovir eliminates transmission entirely, so no other precaution is needed once it is started"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — risk is reduced, never abolished. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Where do plantar warts occur, and what do they form when they cluster?","choices":["On the dorsal foot, and clustered together they form a giant condyloma","On weight-bearing surfaces, and clustered together they form a single deep abscess","On the fingers and palms, and clustered together they form a periungual band","On weight-bearing surfaces, and clustered together they form a mosaic wart"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mosaic warts are clustered plantar warts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"Which features separate a fixed drug eruption from erythema multiforme?","choices":["It produces extensive epidermal detachment with mucosal involvement and systemic toxicity","It produces tender nodules on the anterior shins that never go on to ulcerate","It recurs at the same site, leaves residual hyperpigmentation, and has few lesions without three concentric zones","It produces wheals that migrate, blanch fully and resolve within twenty-four hours of first appearing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the three-zone target is what defines an erythema multiforme lesion. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Beyond treating before diagnosis, which two actions does the lecture associate with the naturalistic approach?","choices":["Ruling out the worst-case scenario and following responses to interventions","Calculating pretest probability and applying sensitivity and specificity","Assessing readiness to change and arranging follow-up","Formulating a clinical question and appraising the literature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed under the naturalistic, event-driven approach. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does the deck require of a biopsy for a suspicious nail lesion?","choices":["Prompt referral to a dermatologist experienced in nail-unit biopsy, with the biopsy sampling the correct site","A shave biopsy of the nail plate itself, taken in primary care","Observation with serial photographs for three months before any biopsy is taken or referral made","Removal of the entire digit for histologic assessment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the referral is to someone experienced in the nail unit specifically. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has vitiligo affecting about fifteen per cent of his body surface. He has used topical steroids without benefit. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the same topical steroid for a further six months","Narrow-band ultraviolet B phototherapy, ideally combined with topical therapy","Psoralen plus ultraviolet A, since it is more effective than narrow-band ultraviolet B","Tissue grafting to the affected areas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — phototherapy is first line above five per cent, and combination therapy is described as ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which forms of lentigines are named?","choices":["Lentigo simplex, acral lentigo, agminated lentigo and generalised lentigo","Superficial, nodular, acral lentiginous and lentigo maligna forms","Common, cellular, combined and atypical cellular forms of the lesion","Erythematotelangiectatic, papulopustular, phymatous and ocular forms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they follow a bimodal age distribution. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a clinically typical polymorphous light eruption. Which investigation is described as mandatory before the diagnosis is accepted?","choices":["A urine, stool and red-cell porphyrin screen sent together","Photopatch testing to a standard series of photoallergens","A skin biopsy of a fresh papule taken within the first day","An antinuclear antibody panel including anti-Ro and anti-La"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lupus must be excluded because it can present identically. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"How soon do most patients feel relief after scabies treatment, and how long may symptoms linger?","choices":["No relief until a second course has been given at four weeks","Relief in about three days, though rash and itch may last up to four weeks","Relief within hours, with all symptoms gone within three days","Relief only after six weeks, with itch persisting for several months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — triamcinolone can be used for the residual itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which specimens are taken in serious cellulitis?","choices":["Blood cultures and a skin punch biopsy","A superficial swab of the intact affected skin","Perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence","A potassium hydroxide preparation of the leading edge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside a complete blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which reaction to bedbug bites occurs particularly in sensitised patients?","choices":["A bullous reaction","A necrotic ulcer with an eschar","A serpentine advancing trail","Cramping abdominal pain and spasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the usual reaction is wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is xanthelasma made of, and where does it sit?","choices":["A crowding of sebocytes within an enlarged gland, most commonly on the face","A collection of lipid-laden macrophages forming soft yellow cholesterol plaques, most commonly on the medial eyelids","A collection of keratin within an enclosed epithelial sac, most commonly on the face, scalp and trunk","A collection of mucin extruded from a joint space, most commonly over a distal finger joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and it is asymptomatic. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man wants several waxy stuck-on lesions on his trunk removed because they catch on his clothing. Which options does the deck give?","choices":["Retinoids, cryotherapy or quality-switched laser, since removal here is elective","Excision with a two-millimetre margin, because they are premalignant","Topical imiquimod five nights weekly for six weeks","No intervention is available for symptomatic lesions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary; removal is for cosmetic or symptomatic reasons. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever is on day 4 of illness. The indirect immunofluorescence assay has been sent. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start doxycycline now, since treatment should begin by day 5 and the assay is rarely diagnostic before day 7","Wait for the assay result, since the gold standard test should guide the choice of antibiotic","Start amoxicillin now, since doxycycline should be reserved for confirmed disease","Repeat the assay in three days and treat only if it has become positive by then"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatment must not wait for the serology to return. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Slide 50's diagram maps levels I to V against skin anatomy (this is the system conventionally called the Clark level). Which layers do they correspond to?","choices":["I into the papillary dermis; II into the reticular dermis; III into subcutaneous tissue; IV into muscle; V into bone","I confined to the stratum corneum; II into the epidermis; III into the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the dermis-subcutaneous junction","I confined to the epidermis; II into the papillary dermis; III filling the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue","I confined to the epidermis; II into the reticular dermis; III filling the reticular dermis; IV into the papillary dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the level of invasion is an ANATOMIC layer, unlike Breslow thickness which is a measurement. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is described as the drug of choice for impetigo in children?","choices":["Cephalexin","Doxycycline","Dicloxacillin","Erythromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"The parents of a child with a large congenital naevus on the back ask whether it can be removed. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["It should be treated with cryotherapy or laser rather than surgery in all young children","It depends on melanoma risk, cosmesis and function; the goal is to remove as much as possible while preserving function","It must be excised completely before school age in every case, regardless of any graft considerations","It should never be excised, since surgery on a congenital naevus increases the risk of melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — where there is little skin for a graft site, observation may be the better option. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"The lecture contrasts an intuitive system of thinking with what alternative?","choices":["A system that applies only to unstable patients presenting to emergency medicine","A system that avoids all use of probability","A system driven entirely by patient preference","A more deliberate and controlled thought process using logic and probabilities"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs quick, automatic thinking against a deliberate, controlled process that uses logic and probabilities. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man reports generalised itching and rough, tight skin each winter, worst on the shins. Examination shows fine scale with a few shallow fissures and scratch marks, without erythematous plaques. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Apply a keratolytic containing urea to the fissured areas first","Take short lukewarm showers with a gentle fragrance-free cleanser only where needed, and apply a thick ointment or cream within minutes of bathing","Take long hot showers each morning to hydrate the skin thoroughly, then apply a light lotion once the skin has completely dried","Use an antibacterial soap over the whole body twice daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hot water and detergents are among the causes, and prompt moisturising after bathing is the key manoeuvre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 153"},{"q":"What is the mortality of toxic epidermal necrolysis, and what predicts it at presentation?","choices":["Around 50%, predicted by the presence of the Nikolsky sign","Up to 30 to 35%, predicted by the SCORTEN score calculated within 24 hours","Between 1 and 5%, predicted by the extent of mucosal involvement","Under 1%, predicted by the number of drugs the patient was taking"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and SCORTEN is repeated on day three. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What is intralesional methotrexate used for in keratoacanthoma, and how does it work?","choices":["Given topically over several months; it slows the turnover of sebocytes within the enlarged sebaceous gland","Given before excision to reduce the size of the lesion; it inhibits deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis in actively dividing cells","Given after excision in order to prevent recurrence; it inhibits fibroblast proliferation within the healing wound bed","Given instead of excision in frail patients; it induces microvascular thrombosis within the vessels feeding the lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a chemotherapeutic agent used as a surgical adjunct. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has a translucent skin-coloured papule over the distal interphalangeal joint of her index finger. The nail beyond it has a longitudinal groove. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acrochordon","Digital mucous cyst","Epidermoid cyst","Chronic paronychia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pressure on the nail matrix causes the groove. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"The parent of a child with a single asymptomatic granuloma annulare ring asks whether treatment is needed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is premalignant and should be excised to prevent later transformation","It is contagious to other children and school attendance should be limited","It will persist unchanged for life unless systemic immunosuppressive therapy is started early in the course","It is benign and self-limiting, with about half resolving within two years, so treatment is for cosmesis or discomfort"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and there is no malignant potential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Confirmatory (supplemental) testing for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is highly specific in order to accomplish what?","choices":["Detect as many infected individuals as possible","Shorten the time needed to obtain a result","Identify the virus before antibodies have developed","Minimise false-positive diagnoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states confirmatory supplemental testing is highly specific, minimising false-positive diagnoses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the hypothesis verification stage accomplish?","choices":["It gathers data to monitor the patient's response to treatment","It selects which screening test to order first","It confirms the fit of the diagnostic hypotheses with the assembled data","It generates the first list of possible diagnoses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verification is defined as confirming the fit of the diagnostic hypotheses with the data assembled. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
